151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,8028,897,315,400,87,61,17,488,379,61,17,4779,1380,400,87,3,323,400,88,3,525,1931,5109,1741,429,400,87,61,17,488,379,61,17,284,220,19,4130,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the minimum value of $x^2 + y^2,$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers such that $x^2 + y^2 = 4xy.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"c4c5bb3ff842d86af7f98f5eb6d1333e9768b5a84d15989f37f810019a40078d"},"seconds":43.15900444984436,"seed":626539149099774419,"stop":"eos","text":"Given the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 4xy\\), we aim to find the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\).\n\nFirst, we rearrange the given equation:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 - 4xy = 0\n\\]\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\\[\nx^2 - 4xy + y^2 = 0\n\\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for the quadratic expression \\(x^2 - 4xy + y^2\\):\n\\[\nx^2 - 4xy + y^2 = (x - 2y)^2 - 3y^2\n\\]\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n(x - 2y)^2 - 3y^2 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n(x - 2y)^2 = 3y^2\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nx - 2y = \\pm \\sqrt{3} y\n\\]\n\nWe now solve for \\(x\\) in terms of \\(y\\):\n1. \\(x - 2y = \\sqrt{3} y\\)\n\\[\nx = (2 + \\sqrt{3}) y\n\\]\n\n2. \\(x - 2y = -\\sqrt{3} y\\)\n\\[\nx = (2 - \\sqrt{3}) y\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute these values back into the expression \\(x^2 + y^2\\) to find the minimum value.\n\nFirst, consider \\(x = (2 + \\sqrt{3}) y\\):\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 = ((2 + \\sqrt{3}) y)^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (2 + \\sqrt{3})^2 y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (4 + 4\\sqrt{3} + 3) y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (7 + 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (8 + 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2\n\\]\n\nNext, consider \\(x = (2 - \\sqrt{3}) y\\):\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 = ((2 - \\sqrt{3}) y)^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (2 - \\sqrt{3})^2 y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (4 - 4\\sqrt{3} + 3) y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (7 - 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (8 - 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2\n\\]\n\nTo find the minimum value, we compare \\((8 + 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2\\) and \\((8 - 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2\\). Clearly, \\((8 - 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2\\) is smaller.\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{8 - 4\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{8 - 4\\sqrt{3}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Given the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 4xy\\), we aim to find the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\).\n\nFirst, we rearrange the given equation:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 - 4xy = 0\n\\]\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\\[\nx^2 - 4xy + y^2 = 0\n\\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for the quadratic expression \\(x^2 - 4xy + y^2\\):\n\\[\nx^2 - 4xy + y^2 = (x - 2y)^2 - 3y^2\n\\]\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n(x - 2y)^2 - 3y^2 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n(x - 2y)^2 = 3y^2\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nx - 2y = \\pm \\sqrt{3} y\n\\]\n\nWe now solve for \\(x\\) in terms of \\(y\\):\n1. \\(x - 2y = \\sqrt{3} y\\)\n\\[\nx = (2 + \\sqrt{3}) y\n\\]\n\n2. \\(x - 2y = -\\sqrt{3} y\\)\n\\[\nx = (2 - \\sqrt{3}) y\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute these values back into the expression \\(x^2 + y^2\\) to find the minimum value.\n\nFirst, consider \\(x = (2 + \\sqrt{3}) y\\):\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 = ((2 + \\sqrt{3}) y)^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (2 + \\sqrt{3})^2 y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (4 + 4\\sqrt{3} + 3) y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (7 + 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (8 + 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2\n\\]\n\nNext, consider \\(x = (2 - \\sqrt{3}) y\\):\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 = ((2 - \\sqrt{3}) y)^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (2 - \\sqrt{3})^2 y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (4 - 4\\sqrt{3} + 3) y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (7 - 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2 + y^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= (8 - 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2\n\\]\n\nTo find the minimum value, we compare \\((8 + 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2\\) and \\((8 - 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2\\). Clearly, \\((8 - 4\\sqrt{3}) y^2\\) is smaller.\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{8 - 4\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:580"}
